    <header><h1 id="title">IPFS Principles</h1><p id="last-modified"><time datetime="2023-03-28T00:00:00.000Z">28 March 2023</time></p><div class="ipseity-maturity"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/status-reliable-green.svg?style=flat-square" height="20" alt="status: reliable"></div><dl><dt>History</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/ipfs/specs/commits/main/src/architecture/principles.md">Commit History</a></dd><dt>Feedback</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/ipfs/specs">GitHub ipfs/specs</a><span> (</span><a href="https://github.com/ipfs/specs/blob/main/src/architecture/principles.md">inspect source</a><span>, </span><a href="https://github.com/ipfs/specs/issues/new/choose">open issue</a><span>)</span></dd></dl><div id="abstract"><p>The IPFS stack is a suite of specifications and tools that share two key characteristics:</p>
<ol>
<li>Data is addressed by its contents using an extensible verifiability mechanism, and</li>
<li>Data is moved in ways that are tolerant of arbitrary transport methods.</li>
</ol>
<p>This document provides context and details about these characteristics. In doing so it defines
what is or is not an IPFS implementation. This is a <strong>living document</strong>; it is expected to
change over time as we define more of the principles that guide the architecture of IPFS or
find clearer ways of describing those we have already defined.</p>
</div></header>
